Start x86 User-mode Linux programs in the initial FPU state

User-mode Linux gives every new program the floating-point registers of the
stub process it sampled at boot. That stub inherits the vector registers of
the UML process, which hold leftovers from host string handling such as the
command line and the binary's path, so a new guest program could see host
bytes that differ between two runs of the same profile. x86 Linux starts
every program in the initial FPU state: an empty x87 stack with control word
0x37f, MXCSR 0x1f80 and zeroed vector registers. The sampled state is now
reset the same way. The protection-key register keeps the sampled value,
which is the host's default for new programs.

This patch is Linux-kernel code (GPL-2.0), carried as a kernel diff under
consonance/harmony-linux/linux/patches/ per the repository's kernel-patch
exception.

---
--- a/arch/um/os-Linux/start_up.c
+++ b/arch/um/os-Linux/start_up.c
@@ -163,6 +163,30 @@
 		exec_fp_regs = malloc(host_fp_size);
 		memcpy(exec_regs, regs->gp, sizeof(exec_regs));
 		memcpy(exec_fp_regs, regs->fp, host_fp_size);
+#ifdef __x86_64__
+		/*
+		 * x86 Linux starts every program in the initial FPU state. The
+		 * FXSAVE image holds the control word at byte 0, MXCSR at 24 and
+		 * its mask at 28; bytes 464-511 describe the signal frame and the
+		 * XSAVE header's component bits start at 512.
+		 */
+		{
+			unsigned char *fx = (unsigned char *)exec_fp_regs;
+			unsigned short cwd = 0x37f;
+			unsigned int mxcsr = 0x1f80;
+			unsigned long long components;
+
+			memset(fx, 0, 24);
+			memcpy(fx, &cwd, sizeof(cwd));
+			memcpy(fx + 24, &mxcsr, sizeof(mxcsr));
+			memset(fx + 32, 0, 464 - 32);
+			if (host_fp_size >= 512 + sizeof(components)) {
+				memcpy(&components, fx + 512, sizeof(components));
+				components &= 1ULL << 9;
+				memcpy(fx + 512, &components, sizeof(components));
+			}
+		}
+#endif
 
 		munmap(seccomp_test_stub_data, sizeof(*seccomp_test_stub_data));
 
